 at  Columbia University to take some of the  amazing properties of say natural  organic growth like the growth of growth  of these biofilms about about the size  of a quarter and apply some techniques  of computations such as machine learning  and computer vision to try to understand  what they're doing and encapsulate it in  a computer model and that's exciting for  me because once we can do that once we  can have a computer model even if it's a  rough computer model of what's happening  with natural biological growth then we  can potentially start using it as part  of a design ecosystem and I'm going to  kind of skip this slide to start with  three approaches that we have been  experimenting with in our studio that we  think are particularly relevant and  interesting and useful kind of in this  context in this context of using nature  using live organisms in some cases a  version of editing nature in order to  make kind of real applications so  they're going to be three approaches and  the first one is bio computation and by  that I mean using actual living  organisms as tiny computers to solve a  problem in a different context and often  at a different scale and this project  started with the real world commissioned  by the airplane manufacturer Airbus to  redesign kind of their process for  design itself for making airplanes but  starting of course with a kind of  inquiry in an incremental way with this  component you see here in red  called a partition and of course  everyone's seen this component many  times and in some ways it's one of the  most boring components of an airplane  but it actually is very challenging to  design from a structural engineering  point of view and so you can see here a  crash test with crash test dummies of  the certification process for the  partition that's flying today in Airbus  planes and just by kind of watching the  response of those crash test dummies to  the forces you get the sense that in  4/10 of a second this is the amount of  force that this panel has to withstand  and it's it's worth noting that it's not  only a lot of force that the panel has  to withstand but there are a lot of  geometric constraints that make this a  hard problem to solve in other words you  can only attach this panel to the  fuselage and four points to at the top  and two at the bottom there has to be  the fold down cabin a tendency which you  saw in the previous slide and that has  to be hanging off of the panel just on  one side so that's a very a symmetrical  load which could be a challenge the  panel can only be about one inch thick  and to top it off you have to be able to  remove that Center area the smaller  shape embedded there because if people  get injured these days in the seats the  seating area of an airplane you have to  be able to put the injured person on a  stretcher and bring them around the  corner out through the door that doesn't  work with the normal turning radius of  the walls like right next to each other  so you have to be able to remove a slot  from the wall I mean it sounds simple  but that's exactly where a structural  engineer would want to place some of the  heaviest structure so it's at this point  that we turned to a version of bio  computing and we used this fairly famous  example of slime mold growth and to me  one of the fascinating things about  slime mold growth is that it creates a  an adaptive Network that is both  efficient and redundant so slime mold is  starting in that center area as you can  see in the video it's growing out too  reach sources of food which are those  white dots and it's doing that in a way  that's efficient so it uses a small  amount of connective material parts of  itself its own body to connect the dots  but it's also redundant in that if you  remove one of the paths typically it can  route around the problem and although  it's an entirely different material and  system we thought this 2-dimensional  adaptive Network that's efficient and  redundant could be a good way to solve a  more or less two dimensional structural  problem so we created a kind of custom  algorithm loosely modeled on the slime  mold growth used a version of  evolutionary computing or a kind of  artificial version of evolution in the  computer to explore many design options  and ultimately arrive at something  that's very different than what standard  engineering approach would create part  of the power of this approach and of  using the computer is that you can  generate evaluate and evolve literally  thousands and tens of thousands of  design options they're shown in the  previous slide as their geometry but we  also have these very large data sets and  we can use some techniques of data  analysis to color code the designs by  their type of design almost like their  digital DNA that creates the kind of  designs you can sort them according to  your two goals so in this case we want  to be low on the x axis and on the y  axis near the origin point to minimize  the amount of weight of the object and  also minimize the displacement under  stress you can hone in on the  mathematically best set of designs  through again other kind of computer  techniques and ultimately this allows us  to use a variety of techniques starting  with what we call a kind of biological  algorithm  derived from slime mold but then  extending to some other types of  computation and engineering to create a  new version of designing lighter weight  airplane parts just like natural systems  we can apply this  multiple scales so without going into  too much detail I'll just note that we  start with a kind of problem description  on the left the shape that we have to  work with we create a network of these  thin gray lines in the middle but then  everything gray line can in itself be  made up of a bunch of tiny red lines so  we can reapply the optimization process  at a different scale so here's a  different view of that you see that we  create and first these just kind of  standard profile bars and then a kind of  micro lattice of tiny bars that make up  those bigger bars as I mentioned this is  a real-world project and we have worked  with our collaborators that are bused to  create now what is the largest metal 3d  printed airplane component which is  designed to be certified and fly in the  plane so we're trying to push some  limits of techniques in design but also  use all of the constraints of real-world  materials production and certification  you know here are some of the components  printed on the bed of a 3d printer and  here's the way that it will kind of  assemble with the very thin cover on the  front and ultimately the reason we're  we're doing this and the reason that  Airbus is kind of investing in this kind  of research is that they want to reduce  the weight of airplane parts that  translates to a reduction in the fuel  consumption which obviously saves money  but it also saves on carbon emissions  and that's a big concern for Airbus and  the whole aerospace industry right now  so compared to the existing partition on  the left our partition weighs about 50  percent less and it's just as strong  even a little bit stronger we have  created this in full-scale and it's  about to undergo the dramatic 16g crash  test in a couple of months  the second approach that I want to talk  about is biosensing so the first one bio  computing this one biosensing and this  one means using actual living organisms  we just heard some great versions of it  from Lin but in our case we're using  actual living organisms so far without  any gene editing or bioengineering but  we think it this technique could be  enhanced potentially by this kind of  engineering but using actual living  organisms in order to sense conditions  of their environment and respond  accordingly and the context for this  project is a public interface to water  quality so this is a photograph of an  early installation that we did of a  network of floating lights you can see  them in blue here right off of Manhattan  and you can see the Manhattan Bridge  there and Brooklyn Bridge in the  background the lights change color and  they give the public an interface to the  quality of the water a condition that's  normally invisible but that we think is  important to the life of the city so the  kind of physical basis of the project is  is these six foot long floating tubes  that have lights above water and sensors  below water we have used some of the  materials digital materials of the  so-called Internet of Things sensors and  small microcontrollers attached to LED  lights but recently we've added to the  mix actual living biosensors and by this  I mean live muscles and as you probably  know and as you can see here in this  video  muscles open and close their shells a  small amount as part of their natural  living conditions so you can see these  muscles kind of pulsing around a little  bit in this sped up video but what's  interesting to me is that it turns out  that the rate and the amount that  muscles open and close their shells is  an incredibly sensitive and  sophisticated detector of water  pollution and so if you  attach a $2 Hall effect sensor to one  half of a mussels shell and an  inexpensive magnet to the other half  then you can get a digital signal based  on this very complex biological function  and you can basically harness a mussel  to be a detector of water pollution that  in some ways is better than our kind of  most sophisticated single purpose  digital sensors so we can get a mussel  sensor for a couple dollars and it could  be better than a $10,000 dissolved  oxygen sensor so that's certainly an  interesting way to maybe save money and  design but what's more interesting to me  is that it kind of represents an example  of the possibility that we can combine  artificial intelligence with natural  intelligence in other words we can  combine the best the computer has to  offer the holy grail of AI with kind of  evolved intelligence that already exists  around us in the natural world and we  could think of using them each as part  of our design palette and potentially  hybridizing them and to me that as a  designer that opens up a whole new world  of possibilities you know much beyond  this project for ways that we could  approach design problems so back to this  installation here you see our floating  network of lights this was an early  prototype installation in the East River  in the context of other lights in the  city and here you see an example of a  kind of tipping point in the data so  this is the kind of interface we'll  offer to the public a kind of warm  reddish quality means that the water is  a little worse than it was a week ago  and a blue color means the reverse so  that was just a tipping point in the  data that we captured there and like  many of our projects we create kind of  quick prototypes to test ideas but this  particular prototype has grown into a  commission to do a larger scale  permanent installation and commissioned  by the city of New York and so this is  going to go into construction in the  late summer and my third and final  example and approach is biomanufacturing  and this one means using actual living  organisms as tiny factories to make the  building blocks of our buildings and  cities and objects and I'm going to show  now a video that we made as an entry  into a competition this is an  architectural competition for the Museum  of Modern Art and MoMA ps1 to design a  structure in their courtyard and this  was our vision inspired some of by some  of the examples of other people in this  room and some of the work that Lynne had  mentioned  and  yes  [Music]  so the idea of the project and the  reason I'm showing this video is this  kind of encapsulated the idea before we  started to encounter the constraints of  of actually making it real the idea of  the project was to use biomanufacturing  to use a living organism as a way to  make building blocks but more than that  it was also to see if we could create a  new approach to design and construction  with almost no waste almost no energy  required and almost no carbon emissions  and also an approach that would be  biodegradable and I think we'll will see  some of the similarities to the  materials and the drone that was  mentioned earlier but at this point our  idea was that maybe we could start with  something like the Earth's carbon cycle  this endless healthy loop of growth and  decay and regrowth and in contrast to  typical architectural approaches maybe  we could take low value raw materials we  could take waste rather than high value  raw material maybe we could spend a very  small amount of energy converting raw  material into building blocks instead of  typically spending a huge amount of  energy to do that maybe we could create  a normal typical building and then at  the end of the useful life of that  building would turn all of the matter  all of the physical stuff of that  building back to the carbon cycle  instead of putting our construction  waste in landfills for hundreds or  thousands of years as we typically do  how could we possibly do that it seems  kind of too good to be true especially  today but we used this which has already  been described a couple times in these  events and this is a microscope video of  the growth of mycelium so this is the  kind of filament like branching  structure of fungi as you know sometimes  as the root like structure of mushrooms  and it turns out that you can take this  a beautiful natural process and take  this material of mycelium and combine it  with agricultural waste not the  high-value part of Agriculture so not  something like the corn kernels but the  low value part of agriculture like the  chopped up corn stalks pack it into a  mold of almost any shape and in about  five days which you see here sped up it  will create a solid object it will grow  into a solid object and you can  potentially use those kind of solid  objects for a variety of things so this  is some video footage of eco ativ  startup in upstate New York that is  creating mycelium based materials for  use in packaging and we approached eco  ativ and collaborated with them to  create a new kind of architectural brick  out of this material since nobody had  made large-scale outdoor architecture  out of this material before we had to do  an incredible amount of testing here's  one of our bricks being crushed with a  hundred thousand pounds of force at a  testing lab at Columbia University we  also tested small assemblies of bricks  and we were doing this in part to learn  about this somewhat unknown material but  we were also doing it just based on the  practicality of getting the project done  in other words as we went to our  structural engineers at erä  our great collaborators on the on the  project one of the first things we  learned and it seems obvious of course  is that their proprietary structural  engineering software and for that matter  any structural engineering software in  the world doesn't have a drop-down menu  item for mushroom bricks when you're  specifying what the material is that  you're working with so we had to develop  that that material profile through  testing we also had some challenges in  creating a complex form with this  material in part because you can't cut  mycelium bricks on-site to make  specific shapes that will compromise  their durability so we created some  custom computer algorithms to figure out  how to solve basically a fitting problem  and a stacking problem we had to fit a  certain number of fixed size bricks onto  courses of different sizes as you go up  in the structure and we had to make sure  every brick overlapped with every with  two bricks below it with at least two  inches of overlap and that was a problem  that was easy to describe but difficult  to solve with this complex geometry when  we arrived on site to actually make this  for real we had done a lot of research a  lot of testing solved a lot of problems  but there were still some things that we  that we had to figure out in the field  and at this point we brought into our  research on materials and biology and  computation a kind of ecosystem of  intelligence and labor in other words we  had Columbia University graduate  students in architecture who know a lot  about computation and form and New York  City brick Masons who know a lot about  stacking objects and each of them had a  different experience and expertise but  none of them had made this kind of thing  in this way before so there was a really  interesting layer of problem-solving and  labor and intelligence in the project as  well so as I'm wrapping up here here's a  photo of the final structure in the  context of the typical glass and steel  buildings of Manhattan in the background  and they traditional clay brick  buildings of MoMA ps1 our project was in  many ways a kind of test at a medium  scale of this idea the structure was  about 41 feet tall and it used 10,000  bricks so this was a test like could we  really make something out in the world  like this would it work but in addition  to being a kind of technical  experiment to test the technical  performance of the material it was a  kind of creative experiment or an  atmospheric experiment as designers we  were curious what it would be like to  stand inside of this structure what  be the qualities of light and shadow and  pattern what would be the experience of  looking at material that was rough and  organic and slightly unfamiliar in the  foreground but having it disappear into  something that looked more knowable and  familiar in the background and finally  the ultimate test of these installations  at MoMA ps1 which happen every summer  now for the past 15 years is their  ability to host a party because every  Saturday 5,000 people come to this  courtyard to hear experimental  electronic music and this was the first  Saturday which was a kind of thrilling  but terrifying moment for us when this  unknown experiment and this new approach  to materials was literally climbed on  and touched and smelled and scratched by  the general public but in the end that  was totally fitting for us because this  idea of bringing a new experiment in  architecture and sometimes in biology  out into the world it's it's fitting for  us that it would happen in public in  culture in society not merely kind of in  our own studio and not merely a kind of  hidden corner of a construction lot the  way a lot of facade prototypes are  created in the world of architecture and  of course there was a kind of life of  its own that it took on in a kind of  public reaction and discussion through  new social media technologies part of  the idea of the project was to see if we  might be able to create a local  ecosystem of materials and energy and  flows and then finally what we were most  interested in the project was the  ability to deconstruct it at the end of  the summer  take all of those bricks crumble them  into smaller pieces and combine them  with yard waste and food scraps and  bacteria and worms and then in 60 days  true to the promise and idea of the  project all of that stuff all of that  physical matter that made this temporary  building was returned to soil which we  then gave to the city for tree planting  and even community gardens the community  gardens aspect shows that it's high  enough quality soil and non-toxic enough  to grow food out of and so I guess you  know to conclude the the big idea out of  the project that we're taking forward is  that maybe as architects and designers  we should think of designing to  disappear as much as we typically think  of designing to appear thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

