building blocks but more than that  it was also to see if we could create a  new approach to design and construction  with almost no waste almost no energy  required and almost no carbon emissions  and also an approach that would be  biodegradable and I think we'll will see  some of the similarities to the  materials and the drone that was  mentioned earlier but at this point our  idea was that maybe we could start with  something like the Earth's carbon cycle  this endless healthy loop of growth and  decay and regrowth and in contrast to  typical architectural approaches maybe  we could take low value raw materials we  could take waste rather than high value  raw material maybe we could spend a very  small amount of energy converting raw  material into building blocks instead of  typically spending a huge amount of  energy to do that maybe we could create  a normal typical building and then at  the end of the useful life of that  building would turn all of the matter  all of the physical stuff of that  building back to the carbon cycle  instead of putting our construction  waste in landfills for hundreds or  thousands of years as we typically do  how could we possibly do that it seems  kind of too good to be true especially  today but we used this which has already  been described a couple times in these  events and this is a microscope video of  the growth of mycelium so this is the  kind of filament like branching  structure of fungi as you know sometimes  as the root like structure of mushrooms  and it turns out that you can take this  a beautiful natural process and take  this material of mycelium and combine it  with agricultural waste not the  high-value part of Agriculture so not  something like the corn kernels but the  low value part of agriculture like the  chopped up corn stalks pack it into a  mold of almost any shape and in about  five days which you see here sped up it  will create a solid object it will grow  into a solid object and you can  potentially use those kind of solid  objects for a variety of things so this  is some video footage of eco ativ  startup in upstate New York that is  creating mycelium based materials for  use in packaging and we approached eco  ativ and collaborated with them to  create a new kind of architectural brick  out of this material since nobody had  made large-scale outdoor architecture  out of this material before we had to do  an incredible amount of testing here's  one of our bricks being crushed with a  hundred thousand pounds of force at a  testing lab at Columbia University we  also tested small assemblies of bricks  and we were doing this in part to learn  about this somewhat unknown material but  we were also doing it just based on the  practicality of getting the project done  in other words as we went to our  structural engineers at erä  our great collaborators on the on the  project one of the first things we  learned and it seems obvious of course  is that their proprietary structural  engineering software and for that matter  any structural engineering software in  the world doesn't have a drop-down menu  item for mushroom bricks when you're  specifying what the material is that  you're working with so we had to develop  that that material profile through  testing we also had some challenges in  creating a complex form with this  material in part because you can't cut  mycelium bricks on-site to make  specific shapes that will compromise  their durability so we created some  custom computer algorithms to figure out  how to solve basically a fitting problem  and a stacking problem we had to fit a  certain number of fixed size bricks onto  courses of different sizes as you go up  in the structure and we had to make sure  every brick overlapped with every with  two bricks below it with at least two  inches of overlap and that was a problem  that was easy to describe but difficult  to solve with this complex geometry when  we arrived on site to actually make this  for real we had done a lot of research a  lot of testing solved a lot of problems  but there were still some things that we  that we had to figure out in the field  and at this point we brought into our  research on materials and biology and  computation a kind of ecosystem of  intelligence and labor in other words we  had Columbia University graduate  students in architecture who know a lot  about computation and form and New York  City brick Masons who know a lot about  stacking objects and each of them had a  different experience and expertise but  none of them had made this kind of thing  in this way before so there was a really  interesting layer of problem-solving and  labor and intelligence in the project as  well so as I'm wrapping up here here's a  photo of the final structure in the  context of the typical glass and steel  buildings of Manhattan in the background  and they traditional clay brick  buildings of MoMA ps1 our project was in  many ways a kind of test at a medium  scale of this idea the structure was  about 41 feet tall and it used 10,000  bricks so this was a test like could we  really make something out in the world  like this would it work but in addition  to being a kind of technical  experiment to test the technical  performance of the material it was a  kind of creative experiment or an  atmospheric experiment as designers we  were curious what it would be like to  stand inside of this structure what  be the qualities of light and shadow and  pattern what would be the experience of  looking at material that was rough and  organic and slightly unfamiliar in the  foreground but having it disappear into  something that looked more knowable and  familiar in the background and finally  the ultimate test of these installations  at MoMA ps1 which happen every summer  now for the past 15 years is their  ability to host a party because every  Saturday 5,000 people come to this  courtyard to hear experimental  electronic music and this was the first  Saturday which was a kind of thrilling  but terrifying moment for us when this  unknown experiment and this new approach  to materials was literally climbed on  and touched and smelled and scratched by  the general public but in the end that  was totally fitting for us because this  idea of bringing a new experiment in  architecture and sometimes in biology  out into the world it's it's fitting for  us that it would happen in public in  culture in society not merely kind of in  our own studio and not merely a kind of  hidden corner of a construction lot the  way a lot of facade prototypes are  created in the world of architecture and  of course there was a kind of life of  its own that it took on in a kind of  public reaction and discussion through  new social media technologies part of  the idea of the project was to see if we  might be able to create a local  ecosystem of materials and energy and  flows and then finally what we were most  interested in the project was the  ability to deconstruct it at the end of  the summer  take all of those bricks crumble them  into smaller pieces and combine them  with yard waste and food scraps and  bacteria and worms and then in 60 days  true to the promise and idea of the  project all of that stuff all of that  physical matter that made this temporary  building was returned to soil which we  then gave to the city for tree planting  and even community gardens the community  gardens aspect shows that it's high  enough quality soil and non-toxic enough  to grow food out of and so I guess you  know to conclude the the big idea out of  the project that we're taking forward is  that maybe as architects and designers  we should think of designing to  disappear as much as we typically think  of designing to appear thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

